syntax: parse further subscripts with bash, and in ksh93 array elements

Bash has no multidimensional arrays, but bash -n accepts every one of

	a[1][2]=x
	declare a[i][j]=x
	echo "${a[1][2]}"
	m=([1][2]=x)

and only running them shows the mistake: the first runs a command named
a[1][2]=x, declare fails with "not a valid identifier", the expansion
fails with "bad substitution", and the array element is stored as the
plain string [1][2]=x. The parser rejected all four, so a script that
held one lost the rest of its statement, and tools could not point at
the second subscript as the mistake it is.

With LangBash and LangBats, the further subscripts after the first one
of an assignment, a parameter expansion or an array element are now
parsed into the Elems field that LangKsh93 already uses for a[i][j].
ArrayElem gains that field too, which LangKsh93 also fills now, since
ksh93 accepts multidimensional elements such as ([1][2]=x).

Reproducer, before this change: `a[1][2]=x` with LangBash failed with
"`a[b]` must be followed by `=`".

